{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "0014", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\Mor_\\mathcal{C}(x, y)$", "MATH_0002": "$\\phi : x \\to y$", "MATH_0003": "$x$", "MATH_0004": "$y$", "MATH_0005": "$\\phi$", "MATH_0006": "$\\psi$", "MATH_0007": "$\\phi$", "MATH_0008": "$\\psi$", "MATH_0009": "$\\phi \\circ \\psi$", "MATH_0010": "$(\\text{Ob}, \\text{Arrows}, s, t, \\circ)$", "TEXTIT_0001": "{\\it source}", "TEXTIT_0002": "{\\it target}", "TEXTIT_0003": "{\\it composable}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\noindent\n", "suffix": "\n\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:d830e335f197f4fdbb009af56c9b5310a5519b868522085fff93f6bc91acea48", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:58322e61f87b7de224eea8111940325c13e28ef7c2c08da4bd97cd75011548c3", "source_text": "It is customary to require all the morphism sets <MATH_0001> to be disjoint. In this way a morphism <MATH_0002> has a unique <TEXTIT_0001> <MATH_0003> and a unique <TEXTIT_0002> <MATH_0004>. This is not strictly necessary, although care has to be taken in formulating condition (2) above if it is not the case. It is convenient and we will often assume this is the case. In this case we say that <MATH_0005> and <MATH_0006> are <TEXTIT_0003> if the source of <MATH_0007> is equal to the target of <MATH_0008>, in which case <MATH_0009> is defined. An equivalent definition would be to define a category as a quintuple <MATH_0010> consisting of a set of objects, a set of morphisms (arrows), source, target and composition subject to a long list of axioms. We will occasionally use this point of view.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:6e7db86cdb624176603f9387330c0e3be3d8edac820ffa92d5287e306825eac1", "unit_id": "tag:0014:p002"}
{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "remark", "parent_tag": "0015", "placeholders": {"CLOSEQUOTE_0001": "''", "MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0002": "$\\Ob(\\mathcal{C})$", "OPENQUOTE_0001": "``"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{remark}\n\\label{categories-remark-big-categories}\n", "suffix": "\n\\begin{enumerate}\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:93fb486ff176f50816061d2fa349064bde052d26d4e3485a44d2e43cfd49f49f", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:5e7b4753bf4306f7b0df1a060eda9ba05430b903d25a3fb2dc20c3c20baae292", "source_text": "Big categories. In some texts a category is allowed to have a proper class of objects. We will allow this as well in these notes but only in the following list of cases (to be updated as we go along). In particular, when we say: <OPENQUOTE_0001>Let <MATH_0001> be a category<CLOSEQUOTE_0001> then it is understood that <MATH_0002> is a set.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:6984d1ea4c48128c1acb947878dcf265e2ab944395ee8fcabcbb2bd8c2482fa2", "unit_id": "tag:0015:statement"}
